   "oldText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. Follow `arc-divider-recolor` for divider-panel tasks and `arc-rectangle-noise` for rectangular frames/fields. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent divider or other defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.\n2. Request a demo when competing rules remain, choosing the one most likely to distinguish them and tracking its cost/cap. Never infer a precise mapping from visual resemblance, an apparent color histogram, or stochastic-looking areas. If demonstrated output contains apparently random/permuted content with no rule to generate its exact values, further samples do not license guessing: distinguish the fixed structure from the undetermined cells, and only submit if the applicable scoring task admits a valid constrained completion; otherwise use the remaining request budget to investigate structural relations, not to invent a random seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify it mechanically: count rows and cells per row, compare the set of non-background coordinates against the input, and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. Track request/attempt caps and costs. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over.",
   "newText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Route divider-panel tasks to `arc-divider-recolor` and rectangular frame/field tasks to `arc-rectangle-noise`; use their procedures to infer and validate those transformations. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks a defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.\n2. Request a demo when competing rules remain, choosing the one most likely to distinguish them and tracking its cost/cap. Never infer a precise mapping from visual resemblance, an apparent color histogram, or stochastic-looking areas. If demonstrated output contains apparently random/permuted content with no rule to generate its exact values, further samples do not license guessing: distinguish the fixed structure from the undetermined cells, and only submit if the applicable scoring task admits a valid constrained completion; otherwise use the remaining request budget to investigate structural relations, not to invent a random seed.\n3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify mechanically: count rows and cells per row, compare the set of non-background coordinates against the input, and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. Track request/attempt caps and costs. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over."
